## Work Loop
1. **Context** — read `knowledge/project/state.md`, and if the branch is behind `origin/main`, also `git diff $(git merge-base HEAD origin/main) origin/main -- knowledge/project/state.md`, since a branch's snapshot is as old as its merge-base; open `knowledge/project/state-evidence.md` only for the items the task touches; invoke the model-invocable `okf-drift:okf-runtime` skill in recall mode with "<terms>"; check `knowledge/playbooks/index.md` for a matching playbook and follow it; run `drift check --changed <path> --format json` for every path you plan to touch and report the concepts it names as candidates that may alarm, plus every planned path no concept covers; run the gate once as a baseline and keep its output, because an alarm already present then is inherited, not yours.
2. **Build** — if you deviate from a playbook or a convention, say so before writing code.
3. **Verify** — run the Verify Checklist in `knowledge/project/conventions.md` item by item — the knowledge gate is its first item — before presenting code.
4. **Grow** — after meaningful work: if what works / is missing / is broken changed, update its one line in `state.md` and its entry in `state-evidence.md`; fix any concept that is now wrong; write a decision or a playbook if one was made or one would have saved a wrong turn; bind every new **code** `code_refs` entry with the drift bootstrap; never bind a high-churn file — build/run scripts, CI workflows, `VERSION`, manifests: list it in `code_refs`, give the concept a `stale_after`, and write a claim whose truth lives there as a dated observation; when the gate flags drift, run `okf-restamp.sh --format-only` first (it settles, with one line in the log entry, a C/Swift-style file whose lines changed only in indentation), then read each remaining anchor and either fix the concept or run `okf-restamp.sh <doc> <anchor> "<what you read>"`, which re-stamps and writes the line in the branch's log entry — never re-stamp code you did not read; log every other bundle change with `okf-log.sh add knowledge '<line>'`; run `okf-tidy.sh` for index rows and `last_updated`, and bump `stale_after` yourself only on a concept you reviewed; these helpers run through `/okf-write`'s pinned launcher; when a `git merge` or `git rebase` stops on a conflicted `drift.lock`, run `okf-lock-merge.py` through the same launcher instead of editing the lock, then review what it reports stale; run `okf-drift:okf-runtime` in gate mode before pushing — CI runs the same gate on the PR, and a FAIL found there costs a round trip.